## What the CPU data shows

The spike was real, and it happened on a server serving almost no users.

| Time (UTC) | `c6s2`, the only server for 25 minutes | Group |
|---|---|---|
| 07:51 to 08:04 | 5 to 8% | 1 server |
| 08:05 to 08:08 | 14%, 19%, 6%, 13% | 1 server |
| 08:09 | 49% | **scale-out at 08:09:03** |
| 08:10 / 08:11 | **93% / 75%** | 3 servers |
| 08:13 | 6% | 3 servers |

- The group grew while CPU was climbing, so the autoscaler did what it's set to do. The question is what made an idle server hit 93% for a few minutes.
- The two new servers used 41% and 49% in their first minute or two, then 5 to 13%. Boot cost is real but short, and it doesn't explain the first spike.
- The earlier journal output, which I cut off by mistake, showed `unattended-upgrade` running at 08:10:42 to 08:10:53. Apache and polkit restarted at 08:10:44, which is what OS package upgrades usually trigger.
- The six earlier scale-outs came about 25 to 62 minutes after a server's creation. The 08:09 one came 25 minutes after `c6s2` booted at 07:44.

**My hypothesis:** each new server runs an automatic OS upgrade some time after boot. It burns the CPU of a shared-core e2-medium, and the autoscaler adds two servers. This is circumstantial: one journal snippet plus timing. I haven't proven it.

